        theory: directory({
          "notes-and-intervals.txt": textFile(
            `NOTES AND INTERVALS\n===================\n\nMusical alphabet: A B C D E F G\nSharps and flats fill the gaps: A A#/Bb B C C#/Db D D#/Eb E F F#/Gb G G#/Ab\n\nNatural half steps: B to C and E to F (no sharp between them).\n\nSemitones and intervals:\n0   unison\n1   minor 2nd\n2   major 2nd\n3   minor 3rd\n4   major 3rd\n5   perfect 4th\n6   tritone (diminished 5th)\n7   perfect 5th\n8   minor 6th\n9   major 6th\n10  minor 7th\n11  major 7th\n12  octave\n\nOn guitar: one fret equals one semitone. Two frets equal a whole tone.`,
          ),
          "major-scale.txt": textFile(
            `THE MAJOR SCALE\n===============\n\nFormula (whole and half steps): W W H W W W H\n\nC major: C D E F G A B C\nG major: G A B C D E F# G\nD major: D E F# G A B C# D\nA major: A B C# D E F# G# A\nE major: E F# G# A B C# D# E\n\nScale degrees: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\nSolfege: do re mi fa sol la ti do\n\nRelative minor starts on the 6th degree. C major and A minor share the same notes.\n\nKey signatures: sharps order F C G D A E B. Flats order is the reverse: B E A D G C F.`,
          ),
          "circle-of-fifths.txt": textFile(
            `CIRCLE OF FIFTHS\n================\n\nClockwise by fifths adds a sharp each step:\nC (0) G (1#) D (2#) A (3#) E (4#) B (5#) F# (6#)\n\nCounter clockwise by fourths adds a flat each step:\nC (0) F (1b) Bb (2b) Eb (3b) Ab (4b) Db (5b)\n\nRelative minors:\nC-Am, G-Em, D-Bm, A-F#m, E-C#m, B-G#m, F-Dm, Bb-Gm, Eb-Cm\n\nUses:\n- Find the key signature quickly\n- Find the I, IV and V chords (neighbours on the circle)\n- Plan modulations and common chord substitutions`,
          ),
          "modes.txt": textFile(
            `MODES OF THE MAJOR SCALE\n========================\n\nIonian      1 2 3 4 5 6 7       major, happy, stable\nDorian      1 2 b3 4 5 6 b7     minor with a bright 6th (Santana, Oye Como Va)\nPhrygian    1 b2 b3 4 5 b6 b7   dark, Spanish flavour\nLydian      1 2 3 #4 5 6 7      dreamy, floating (Satriani)\nMixolydian  1 2 3 4 5 6 b7      bluesy major, rock and blues\nAeolian     1 2 b3 4 5 b6 b7    natural minor\nLocrian     1 b2 b3 4 b5 b6 b7  unstable, rarely used\n\nParent scale trick: D Dorian uses the notes of C major starting on D.\n\nBetter way to learn modes: play them over a drone or a static chord so the character is clear.\n\nQuick uses:\n- Dorian over minor 7th chords\n- Mixolydian over dominant 7th chords\n- Lydian over major 7th chords`,
