        strat: directory({
          "README.md": textFile(
            `FENDER STRATOCASTER GUIDES\n==========================\n\nSetup, maintenance and repair notes for a standard Stratocaster.\n\nKey specs (typical):\n- Scale length: 25.5 in (648 mm)\n- Frets: 21 or 22\n- Fretboard radius: 7.25 in (vintage) or 9.5 in (modern)\n- Pickups: three single coils\n- Controls: master volume, two tones, 5 way switch\n- Bridge: 6 screw vintage synchronised tremolo, or 2 point, or hardtail\n- Strings: 10-46 or 9-42\n\nImportant: specifications differ by model, year and country. Always check the specifics for your guitar.\n\nOrder of setup:\n1. Strings\n2. Truss rod (neck relief)\n3. Tremolo spring balance\n4. Action (saddle height)\n5. Intonation\n6. Pickup height\n7. Check nut and electronics\n\nTry: ls setup/\nls repair/`,
          ),
          setup: directory({
            "01-tools-and-prep.txt": textFile(
              `TOOLS AND PREPARATION\n=====================\n\nTools:\n- Screwdrivers (Phillips #1 and #2)\n- Hex keys in metric and imperial (check which fit your saddles, claw and truss rod)\n- Truss rod wrench for your neck\n- Radius gauge\n- Feeler gauges or a string action ruler\n- Capo\n- Strobe or clip on tuner\n- Wire cutters and string winder\n- Nut files (only if you know what you are doing)\n- Graphite (pencil) or nut lubricant\n- Soft cloth and fretboard cleaner\n\nBefore starting:\n- Put the guitar on a padded surface or a neck rest\n- Check the guitar is in tune and at pitch\n- Note all current measurements first\n- Make small changes, one at a time\n- Let the neck settle between truss rod changes`,
            ),
            "02-changing-strings.txt": textFile(
              `CHANGING STRINGS\n================\n\nOn a Strat with a vintage tremolo, change one string at a time so the bridge keeps tension and stays level.\n\nSteps:\n1. Loosen the old string and remove it\n2. Thread the new string through the tremolo block from the rear\n3. Pull the string through leaving about 2 inches past the tuner post\n4. Wrap 2 to 3 times (wound strings) or 3 to 4 times (plain strings) neatly downward on the post\n5. Tune up and stretch gently\n6. Retune several times until stable\n\nTip: pull each string away from the fretboard a few times, then retune, to settle it in.\n\nClean the fretboard when strings are off. Oil unfinished rosewood sparingly.\n\nStrings: 9-42 for easy bends, 10-46 for fuller tone and more tension. Switching gauge means adjusting the truss rod, springs and intonation.`,
            ),
            "03-truss-rod-neck-relief.txt": textFile(
              `TRUSS ROD AND NECK RELIEF\n=========================\n\nThe truss rod counters string tension. Neck relief is the slight forward bow of the neck.\n\nMeasure relief:\n1. Tune to pitch\n2. Capo at the first fret\n3. Hold the low E string down at the fret where the neck meets the body (around fret 17 to 21)\n4. Look at the gap between the string and the top of fret 7 or 8\n5. A typical target is around 0.010 in (0.25 mm), about the thickness of a thin feeler gauge or a business card edge\n\nAdjust:\n- Too much relief (string too far from fret): tighten the truss rod (clockwise as you look at the nut)\n- Too little or back bow (buzz): loosen the truss rod (anticlockwise)\n\nRules:\n- Quarter turn at most, then wait a few minutes\n- Never force it. If it feels stuck, stop.\n- Detune slightly first if the nut is very tight\n- Do not adjust to fix problems that are not neck related\n\nFender truss rod access is typically at the headstock end on modern necks and at the heel on vintage style necks (requires removing the neck on some models).`,
            ),
            "04-tremolo-spring-balance.txt": textFile(
              `TREMOLO SPRING BALANCE\n======================\n\nThe tremolo is balanced between string tension and the springs in the back cavity.\n\nSpring layouts:\n- 5 springs: more stable, stiffer feel\n- 3 springs in a V shape: more sensitive, easier dive\n\nHow to set the tremolo:\n- Floating: the bridge plate is lifted slightly off the body. Allows pull up as well as dive. Needs good tuning stability.\n- Decked: the plate is flat on the body. Only dives. Most stable.\n\nSetting it to float:\n1. Tune up to pitch\n2. Tighten the claw screws to balance the plate parallel to the body, often with a small gap at the rear edge\n3. Retune and check again\n4. Test with a bend and a dive and check tuning returns\n\nAdd a credit card or small wood shim at the back of the plate while adjusting to keep it level.\n\nTune with the tremolo arm in place if you play with the arm.`,
            ),
            "05-action-and-saddle-height.txt": textFile(
              `ACTION AND SADDLE HEIGHT\n========================\n\nAction is the string height above the frets.\n\nMeasure at the 12th fret, from the top of the fret to the bottom of the string.\n\nTypical starting points for a Strat:\n- Low E (6th string): about 5/64 in (2.0 mm)\n- High E (1st string): about 4/64 in (1.6 mm)\n\nThe saddles should follow the fretboard radius. Use a radius gauge: the strings (not the saddles) should match the curve.\n\nAdjust:\n- Use the small hex screws on each side of each saddle\n- Raise or lower both screws evenly\n- Re-check the neck relief if the tension changes\n\nIf the action is low and you get buzzing:\n- Check relief first\n- Then check for high frets\n- Then raise the saddle slightly\n\nPlay the guitar after adjusting to see how it feels. Personal preference matters.`,
            ),
            "06-nut-check.txt": textFile(
              `NUT CHECK\n=========\n\nThe nut sets string spacing and the height of the strings at the first fret.\n\nCheck the nut slot height:\n1. Fret the string at the 3rd fret\n2. Look at the gap between the string and the top of the first fret\n3. There should be a tiny gap, about the thickness of a sheet of paper (about 0.004 to 0.010 in)\n\nSigns of trouble:\n- Buzz on open strings: slots too low\n- Hard to play in first position, notes sharp: slots too high\n- Pinging sounds when tuning: strings binding in the slots\n\nLubricate slots with graphite from a soft pencil to reduce binding, especially if you use the tremolo a lot.\n\nTip: if the nut needs recutting, take it to a technician or practise on a spare nut. Slots cut too deep cannot be undone without a new nut or filler.\n\nAlso check the string trees (retainers). A roller tree or a lowered tree can reduce friction.`,
            ),
            "07-intonation.txt": textFile(
              `INTONATION\n==========\n\nIntonation makes sure the guitar stays in tune up the neck.\n\nProcess for each string:\n1. Fit fresh strings\n2. Tune the open string precisely\n3. Play the harmonic at the 12th fret (do not press the string)\n4. Play the fretted note at the 12th fret\n5. Compare the two notes with a tuner\n\nResults:\n- Fretted note sharp: saddle is too close to the nut. Move the saddle back (away from the nut) by tightening the intonation screw.\n- Fretted note flat: saddle is too far back. Move the saddle forward (towards the nut) by loosening the screw.\n\nRetune the open string after every adjustment and check again.\n\nTip: Keep string height and neck relief fixed first. Changing them affects intonation.\n\nDo all six strings, then play chords up and down the neck to check by ear.`,
            ),
            "08-pickup-height.txt": textFile(
              `PICKUP HEIGHT\n=============\n\nPickup height affects output, clarity and string pull.\n\nMeasure: hold the low and high E strings down at the last fret. Measure from the bottom of the string to the top of the pole piece.\n\nTypical starting points (adjust by ear):\n- Neck pickup: about 3/32 in on the bass side, 5/64 in on the treble side\n- Middle pickup: about 3/32 in on the bass side, 5/64 in on the treble side\n- Bridge pickup: about 5/64 in on the bass side, 1/16 in on the treble side\n\nThe bass side is set lower (further from the string) to avoid the string vibrating into the magnet.\n\nToo close: harsh, muddy, wavering notes (magnetic pull on the string).\nToo far: weak, thin.\n\nBalance the volume of the three pickups so switching sounds even.\n\nAdjust with the two screws at either end (springs underneath). Adjust treble and bass sides separately for tilt.`,
            ),
            "09-final-checks.txt": textFile(
              `FINAL CHECKS\n============\n\nAfter the setup:\n- Play every fret on every string for buzzing\n- Play open chords and barre chords\n- Bend strings on high frets to check for choking out\n- Test tremolo return to pitch\n- Check the 5 way switch positions\n- Tighten screws (output jack, strap buttons, pickguard)\n- Wipe down the guitar and polish the frets\n\nWrite down the new measurements in the setup log for next time.\n\nRevisit the setup after a few days. New strings will have settled and the neck may have moved.`,
            ),
          }),
          repair: directory({
            "electronics-wiring.txt": textFile(
              `STRAT ELECTRONICS AND WIRING\n============================\n\nStandard layout:\n- 1 master volume (250k pot)\n- 2 tone controls (250k pots), one for neck, one for middle\n- 5 way blade switch\n- 3 single coil pickups\n- Tone capacitor (typically .022 microfarad)\n- Output jack\n\nWiring notes:\n- Use 250k pots for single coils. 500k for humbuckers.\n- All grounds connect together: pot casings, bridge ground wire, and the sleeve of the jack.\n- The jack tip carries signal. The sleeve is ground.\n- The bridge pickup does not go through a tone control by default (this gives it a brighter sound).\n\nPopular mods:\n- Wire the second tone control to the bridge pickup\n- Treble bleed circuit on the volume pot\n- 4 way or 5 way super switch\n- Noiseless or hum cancelling pickups\n- Shielding the cavity with copper tape\n\nSolder carefully. Use a hot iron, quick joints, and rosin core solder. Take a photo before desoldering anything.\n\nLearn about the pickup selector positions:\n1 bridge\n2 bridge and middle\n3 middle\n4 middle and neck\n5 neck`,
            ),
            "scratchy-pots-and-jack.txt": textFile(
              `SCRATCHY POTS AND JACK PROBLEMS\n===============================\n\nCrackling when turning volume or tone controls:\n- Spray contact cleaner into the pot\n- Rotate the pot back and forth several times\n- Replace the pot if cleaning does not help\n\nIntermittent signal or crackling when the cable moves:\n- Tighten the output jack nut\n- Clean the jack with contact cleaner\n- Check the solder joints on the jack\n- Replace the jack if the contact spring is worn\n\nTest with a different cable first.\n\nUse a multimeter to check pot values and continuity.`,
            ),
            "noise-and-hum.txt": textFile(
              `NOISE AND HUM\n=============\n\nSingle coil pickups pick up mains hum. Some hum is normal.\n\nCheck:\n- Hum goes away when you touch the strings or bridge: ground wire issue\n- Hum is worse near lights, computers and amps: interference from nearby gear\n- Hum in positions 2 and 4 is usually lower on a Strat because the pickups are out of phase and hum cancelling\n\nFixes:\n- Make sure the bridge ground wire is connected\n- Shield the control cavity and the back of the pickguard with copper or aluminium tape\n- Use a noise gate or a hum cancelling pedal\n- Swap in noiseless single coil pickups\n- Turn away from the amp or computer screen\n\nAlso check that the cable is shielded and not damaged.`,
            ),
            "fret-buzz-diagnosis.txt": textFile(
              `FRET BUZZ DIAGNOSIS\n===================\n\nFind where the buzz happens:\n- Open strings only: nut slots too low, or neck too flat\n- First few frets: not enough relief or high frets\n- Mid and high frets: low action or uneven frets\n- Only on one string: string seated badly, saddle too low, or a fret issue\n- Buzz only when bending: fret choke (neck too flat or fret edge sharp)\n\nChecks:\n1. Neck relief (see setup notes)\n2. Action\n3. Fret height with a fret rocker\n4. Loose parts (tuning machines, strap buttons, tremolo springs, pickguard screws)\n\nFixes range from a truss rod tweak to a professional fret level and crown. If frets are unevenly worn, take it to a technician.`,
            ),
            "fret-care-and-polish.txt": textFile(
              `FRET CARE AND POLISHING\n=======================\n\nCleaning:\n- Mask off the fretboard edges\n- Use fine steel wool (0000) on maple fretboards with caution, or fret erasers\n- Polish frets with a micro mesh pad or fine polishing compound\n- Wipe away dust\n\nRosewood and ebony: use a little lemon oil or fretboard conditioner once or twice a year.\nMaple (finished): wipe clean with a damp cloth, no oil needed.\n\nFret problems:\n- Fret sprout: the ends stick out of the board (usually from dry weather). Humidify the guitar and have a technician dress the edges.\n- Fret wear: flat spots, buzzing. Needs a level, crown and polish.\n- Lifted fret: pressed back and glued.\n\nLevelling and crowning require specialised tools. Do not attempt without practice.`,
            ),
            "stripped-screw-holes.txt": textFile(
              `STRIPPED SCREW HOLES\n====================\n\nCommon places: pickguard, strap buttons, neck plate, tuner screws.\n\nFix:\n1. Remove the screw\n2. Dip a toothpick or small wooden dowel in wood glue\n3. Push it into the hole and break it off flush\n4. Let it dry for several hours\n5. Re-drill a small pilot hole if needed and reinstall the screw\n\nFor neck screws, use hardwood dowel and make sure the neck is seated tightly with no gaps.\n\nSlightly waxing the threads of screws (candle wax) helps them go in easily and reduces the risk of stripping.`,
            ),
            "neck-pocket-and-neck-angle.txt": textFile(
              `NECK POCKET AND NECK ANGLE\n==========================\n\nA Strat neck is bolted on with four screws and a neck plate.\n\nSigns of loose neck:\n- Neck moves when pushed side to side\n- Tuning problems\n- Gaps in the neck pocket\n- Sound is thin and sustain is poor\n\nFixes:\n- Tighten the neck screws evenly\n- Check for stripped holes\n- If the saddles are too high to get good action, the neck angle might need shimming\n\nShimming (thin paper or veneer in the neck pocket) tilts the neck back to change the angle. A shim at the far end of the pocket (nearest the body) raises the bridge height.\n\nOnly shim if you fully understand the effect. It can be a job for a technician.`,
            ),
            "tuning-stability.txt": textFile(
              `TUNING STABILITY\n================\n\nChecklist when a Strat will not stay in tune:\n\n1. Fresh strings, properly stretched and wound\n2. Tuning machines tight (check the nuts and screws)\n3. Nut slots lubricated and not pinching\n4. String tree lubricated or replaced with a roller\n5. Bridge saddles clean and not sticking\n6. Tremolo springs balanced and properly set\n7. Block and claw screws tightened\n8. Neck screws tight\n9. Environment stable (temperature and humidity)\n10. Lock tuners if necessary\n\nUse a stretching technique after string changes.\n\nTremolo specific: try decking the bridge or adding a fifth spring if you do not use the arm.`,
            ),
            "finish-and-cleaning.txt": textFile(
              `FINISH CARE AND CLEANING\n========================\n\nNitrocellulose finish:\n- Reacts with some rubber (strap hooks, stands). Use nitro safe stands.\n- Wipe with a clean dry cloth. Do not use harsh chemicals.\n- Use a guitar polish designed for nitro.\n\nPolyurethane or polyester finish:\n- Wipe with a microfibre cloth and guitar polish\n\nHardware:\n- Wipe tuning machines and the bridge with a dry cloth\n- Put a tiny drop of oil on the tuning machine gear if it feels gritty\n- Replace the rusty screws\n\nDo not leave the guitar in a hot car or in direct sunlight.\n\nStore the guitar in its case with the strings slightly loosened for long term storage and keep a humidity pack in the case.`,
            ),
            "pickup-swap.txt": textFile(
              `SWAPPING PICKUPS\n================\n\n1. Take photos of the wiring\n2. Loosen the strings and remove the pickguard\n3. Desolder the pickup leads (positive from the switch, ground at the back of the volume pot)\n4. Move the new pickups into the pickguard and install the height springs\n5. Solder the leads in the same locations\n6. Test with a screwdriver tapping on the pole pieces before reassembling (you should hear a click)\n7. Reassemble and set the pickup height\n\nMatch the pickups by output and tone. Mixed sets can sound unbalanced.\n\nReverse wound reverse polarity (RWRP) middle pickups give hum cancelling in positions 2 and 4.`,
            ),
            "spring-cavity-and-trem-block.txt": textFile(
              `SPRING CAVITY AND TREMOLO BLOCK\n===============================\n\nThe back cavity contains the claw, springs and the tremolo block.\n\n- Claw is mounted with two screws. Moving it closer to the body increases tension on the springs.\n- Tremolo arm fits into the block, with a small threaded sleeve.\n- Springs come in different thickness (regular, heavy duty)\n\nProblems:\n- Tremolo arm loose: tighten the arm collar or add a small plastic washer\n- Squeaking springs: lubricate lightly or replace\n- Tremolo does not return: check springs, bridge screws, and nut slots\n\nKeep the cover plate on to protect the springs and reduce noise.`,
            ),
          }),
          "maintenance-schedule.txt": textFile(
            `MAINTENANCE SCHEDULE\n====================\n\nEvery play:\n- Wipe down strings and neck\n\nEvery 2 to 4 weeks (depending on playing):\n- Change strings\n- Clean the fretboard\n\nEvery 3 months:\n- Check the neck relief\n- Check tuners and jack\n- Tighten screws\n\nEvery 6 months:\n- Full setup check: action, intonation, pickup height\n- Oil rosewood boards\n\nEvery year:\n- Clean contacts and pots\n- Professional inspection if needed\n\nSeasonal humidity changes affect the neck. If the action changes dramatically in winter or summer, check relief.`,
